      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "The hypothesis links Ca/Si/Al/O stoichiometry to defect density and ion migration activation energy via a percolation threshold. This requires exploring the compositional design space to identify where the percolation threshold occurs and how void density modulates migration barriers. We will systematically vary composition parameters, simulate glass structures, compute void metrics and ion migration barriers, and map the relationship to identify the critical percolation point."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "md_pipeline"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The exploration will reveal a non-monotonic relationship between Ca content and void density, with a critical percolation threshold at ~15-20 mol% Ca where void connectivity maximizes and activation energy for ion migration shows a minimum. The activation energy will decrease with increasing void connectivity up to the percolation threshold, then increase as the network becomes too disrupted."
    },
